<div class='problem-environment problem' id='problem1' numbered=' ' role='article' titletext=' Problem'>  The graph of <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(g\)</span>, a function continuous on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\([0, 4]\)</span>, is shown in the figure. Let <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(x) = \int _0^x g(t) \d t\)</span> for
<span class='mathjax-inline'>\(0 \leq x \leq 4\)</span>.
<div class='center'>
<!-- l. 20 --><p class='noindent'>
</p><!-- l. 21 --><p class='noindent'><img alt='[Picture]' src='q7_soln.online-7fb2afd8b94a740c9a59b19599692c98.svg' /></p></div>
Circle the correct statement about <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1)\)</span>.
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1) = 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1) &lt; 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1) &gt; 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem2' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-6r1'></a>Correct choice: (ii) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1) &lt; 0\)</span>. <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1)\)</span> is the net area of the region bounded by the curve and the
<span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x\)</span>-axis between <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x=0\)</span> and <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x=1\)</span>. On this interval, <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(g(t)\)</span> is negative and therefore, <span class='mathjax-inline'>\( A(1)\)</span> is negative.
</div>
                                                                  

                                                                  
Circle the correct statement about <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1.5)\)</span>.
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1.5) = 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1.5) &lt; 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1.5) &gt; 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem3' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-11r2'></a>Correct choice: (ii) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(1.5) &lt; 0\)</span> The net area on the interval <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((1,1.5)\)</span> is postive and smaller than the
negative net area on the interval <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((0,1)\)</span>. Therefore, the net area on the interval <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((0,1.5)\)</span> is negative.
</div>
Circle the correct statement about <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'(1.5)\)</span>.
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'(1.5) = 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'(1.5) &lt; 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'(1.5) &gt; 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem4' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-16r3'></a>Correct choice: (iii) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'(1.5) &gt; 0\)</span> because <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'(1.5)=g(1.5)\)</span> </div>
Circle the correct expression for <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(\int _1^4 (g(t) + 1) \d t\)</span>.
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(4) + 1\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(4) - A(1)\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(4) - A(1) + 1\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(4) + 3\)</span>;
                                                                  

                                                                  
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(e) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(4) - A(1) + 3\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(f) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem5' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-23r4'></a>Correct choice: (v) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(4) - A(1) + 3\)</span>
<!-- l. 89 --><p class='noindent'>We can split the integral: <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(\int _1^4 (g(t) + 1) \d t = \int _1^4 g(t) \d t +\int _1^4 1 \d t =A(4)-A(1)+3\)</span>
</p><!-- l. 91 --><p class='noindent'>We can visualize this integal by imagining shifting the graph of <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(g\)</span> up one unit. See
figure below. Our area we are looking for consists of the area under the curve <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(g\)</span> on the
interval <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((1,4)\)</span> (the blue region) plus the rectangular region formed below (the purple
region).<br class='newline' />
</p>
<div aria-labelledby='image-alt-1' class='image-environment' role='img'><img alt='PIC' src='./xmPictures/figure3.png' /> </div>
<!-- l. 99 --><p class='noindent'>The area of the blue region is <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(\int _1^4 g(t) \d t= \int _0^4 g(t) \d t- \int _0^1 g(t) \d t=A(4)-A(1)\)</span>.
</p><!-- l. 101 --><p class='noindent'>The area of the purple region is <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(\int _1^4 1 \d t\)</span>. This integral is the area under the line <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(y=1\)</span> on the
interval <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((1,4)\)</span> which is a rectangular region of area <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(3\)</span>.
</p><!-- l. 103 --><p class='noindent'>Combining these, the total area <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(\int _1^4 (g(t) + 1) \d t = A(4)-A(1)+3\)</span>
</p>
</div>
Circle the correct statement about <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(0)\)</span>.
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(0) = 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(0) = 1\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(0 &lt; A(0) &lt; 1\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(0) &gt; 1\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(e) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(0) &lt; 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(f) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem6' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-30r5'></a>Correct choice: (i) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(0) = 0\)</span>. By the definition of <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span>, <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(x)=\int _0^x g(t) \d t\)</span> for <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(0 \le x \le 4\)</span>. So, <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(0)=\int _0^0 g(t) \d t=0\)</span>. </div>
                                                                  

                                                                  
Circle the interval (or intervals) where the function <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> is DECREASING.
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\((0, 1)\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\((1, 2)\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\((2, 4)\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem7' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-35r6'></a>Correct choice: (i) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((0, 1)\)</span>. <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> is decreasing means that the area is decreasing or we are
accumulating negative area. This happens when <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span>’s derivative, <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(g\)</span> is negative.
</div>
Circle the value (or values) where the function <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> attains its MAXIMUM.
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 1\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 2\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 3\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(e) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 4\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(f) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem8' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-42r7'></a>Correct choice: (v) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 4\)</span>. It appears that <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(x)&lt;0\)</span> on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((0,2)\)</span> and <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A(x)&gt;0\)</span> on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((2,4]\)</span>. So, the maximum occurs on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\([2,4]\)</span>.
Since <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'=g\)</span> is postive on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((2,4)\)</span>, and <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> is increasing there, the maximum is attained at
the end point, <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x=4\)</span>. In other words, <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> achieves a maximum when we have the
largest potive area. Because we start with negative area and then continue
to accumulate positive area until <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x=4\)</span>, the maximum occurs at the end point.
</div>
Circle the value (or values) where the function <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> attains its MINIMUM.
                                                                  

                                                                  
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 0\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 1\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 2\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 3\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(e) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 4\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(f) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem9' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-49r8'></a>Correct choice: (ii) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 1\)</span>. <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'=g\)</span> is negative on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((0,1)\)</span> and positive on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((1,4)\)</span>. So <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> is decreasing on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((0,1)\)</span> and
increasing on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((1,4)\)</span>. So the local and absolute maximum occurs at <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x=1\)</span>. In other words, <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span>
achieves a minimum when we have the largest negative area. We start by
accumulating negative area and then switch to accumulating positive area at <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x=1\)</span>.
</div>
Circle the interval (or intervals) where the function <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> is CONCAVE DOWN.
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\((0, 1)\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\((1, 2)\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\((2, 4)\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem10' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-54r9'></a>Correct choice: (iii) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((2, 4)\)</span>. <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> is concave down where <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'=g\)</span> is decreasing. <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(g\)</span> is decreasing on <span class='mathjax-inline'>\((2,4)\)</span>.
</div>
Circle the value (or values) of <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x\)</span> where the function <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A\)</span> has an inflection point.
<dl class='enumerate-enumitem'><dt class='enumerate-enumitem'> 
(a) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 1\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(b) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 2\)</span>;
                                                                  

                                                                  
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(c) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'><span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x = 3\)</span>;
</dd>     
<dt class='enumerate-enumitem'>
(d) 
</dt><dd class='enumerate-enumitem'>none of the previous answers.</dd></dl>
<div class='theorem-like problem-environment explanation' id='problem11' titletext=' explanation'><a id='x1-59r10'></a>Correct choice: (ii) <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(x=2\)</span>. <span class='mathjax-inline'>\(A'=g\)</span> changes from increasing to decreasing. </div>
